    "summary": "Stellar Dark Star>Alligator>Eleven trek, tight New Speedway Boogie",
    "blurb": "The second set rolls nonstop from Uncle John's Band into a soaring Dark Star→Alligator→The Eleven sequence, highlighted by a fiery New Speedway Boogie and soulful Black Peter. Jerry’s SG is on fire with crisp tone while Pigpen adds a relaxed organ texture to the early material. The A+ soundboard capture is crystal‑clear, making this one of the most vivid late‑‘69 performances.",
    "review": "December 30, 1969 at Boston’s Tea Party captured a turning point for the Dead as they dove deep into extended improvisation with astonishing clarity. The first set offers solid blues‑rock fare—Good Lovin’, Mama Tried, New Speedway Boogie and a tight Black Peter—but it’s the second set that defines the night. Uncle John's Band opens with a tender solo from Jerry, segueing into Mason's Children before the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider pairing. From there Dark Star erupts, morphs seamlessly into Alligator, launches into a powerful The Eleven, and spirals through a series of jams that keep the energy soaring to the final “And We Bid You Good Night.” Across all recordings Jerry’s SG is blazing with perfect tone, delivering both scorching leads on the Dark Star suite and a heartfelt solo on Uncle John's Band. Pigpen’s organ provides a laid‑back backdrop that contrasts nicely with the high‑octane jams, while the rhythm section—Mickey and Billy—holds down the groove with steady, propulsive drumming. The Miller soundboard (gd69-12-30.sbd.miller.28469.sbeok.shnf) delivers an A+ mix: crisp, balanced, and virtually free of flaws, making it the definitive source for this legendary performance.",
